  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWeak high pressure builds in from the west tonight, which will \nallow for some clearing to occur along with winds \ndiminishing...leading to enough raditional cooling to generate \npatch fog. After the fog mixes out on Sunday morning, the rest \nof the day should remain dry and quiet. \n\nThe early part of Monday should be mostly quiet as well, but \nthe next round of showers and storms looks to move in from the \nwest later in the day Monday. There is some indication that the \nremnants of a Midwest MCS could be tracking east through the \nsouthern Great Lakes Sunday night/Monday morning and eventually \ninto NY/PA by Monday afternoon. An embedded upper short wave \nalong with this convective system may help sustain the storms as\nthey track to the east. This will require close watching as \nthere could be remnants of an EML associated with this \nconvection as well. \n\nChances for showers and storms Tuesday and Wednesday appear to \nbe on the lower side, but given the warm temperatures and high \nsurface dew points, persistent cyclonic flow and sporadic weak \nshort waves within the flow, any one period cannot be ruled out \ncompletely for thunderstorms. \n\nA higher chance for showers and storms arrives Thursday with \nthe arrival of a deep upper trough and associated stronger \nforcing. The weekend may be cooler and drier if this trough can \nkick east in time.",
